<urn:atm:triage:finding/ARCH-001>
  a triage:Finding ;
  triage:findingId "ARCH-001" ;
  triage:title "new sealed TaskCommandService trait has zero boundary governance" ;
  triage:description "TaskCommandService (crates/atm-core/src/task_command.rs:226) is a new sealed trait, implemented by CoreTaskCommandService (crates/atm-core/src/task_command/service.rs:359), consumed cross-crate by atm-http-runtime. boundaries/atm-core/task-command-service.toml -- explicitly named in the sprint doc's own \"Affected paths\" list -- does not exist anywhere in the repo, confirmed at head 6937c547f. docs/atm-core/boundaries.md has zero prose mentions of TaskCommandService. .just/lint_boundaries.py has no rule enumerating \"every sealed-trait impl has a boundary record.\" Independently corroborated by req-qa (filed as ATM-QA-101) and ruthless-boundary-qa (filed as RBQA-F001 in that reviewer's own numbering, scored Important -- same underlying fact; treated here as Blocking per arch-qa's evidence-backed framing and the sprint doc's own explicit deliverable listing). Note: this repo's phase-az triage directory already has an unrelated finding file named RBQA-F001.ttl from AZ.1's QA-1 pass -- do not reuse that filename; this finding is filed as ARCH-001 with ATM-QA-101 and the reviewer's own RBQA-F001 label recorded here only as corroborating source ids, not as this record's finding id. Fix: add boundaries/atm-core/task-command-service.toml naming CoreTaskCommandService as the sole permitted impl site, matching the pattern used for TaskStore/AsyncTaskLedgerReader/AsyncTaskMutationStore, plus matching prose in docs/atm-core/boundaries.md. Source: QA-1 on PR #1382 (https://github.com/randlee/atm-core/pull/1382#issuecomment-5612459494), corroborated by req-qa/ruthless-boundary-qa/arch-qa." ;
